const e=[{id:1,icon:"Snowflake",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Winter Safety",subtitle:"Protecting yourself from the cold and the dangers of winter",summary:"Winter is a beautiful season, but it also has its dangers: cold, snow, ice, slipping and poisonous gas from heaters. We need to know how to protect ourselves and our families.",blocks:[{heading:"Heating Your Home Safely",body:`Use gas, kerosene and electric heaters correctly and according to the manufacturer's instructions.
• Keep an opening for fresh air in the room, because gas and kerosene heaters produce the poisonous gas carbon monoxide (CO).
• Do not leave a heater switched on while you sleep.`},{heading:"Protection from the Cold",body:"Wear warm clothes in layers. Cover your head, hands and feet well. When the weather is very cold, do not stay outside for a long time."},{heading:"Driving Safely on Snow and Ice",body:"Drive slowly on snow and ice. Increase the distance between your car and the car in front. Use winter tires or snow chains."},{heading:"Preventing Frozen Pipes",body:"Water pipes can freeze and burst in the cold. Wrap and insulate outdoor pipes. On very cold nights, let a little water run slowly from the faucet."}],tips:["Install a CO (carbon monoxide) alarm at home","Turn off the gas heater before you go to sleep","Wear clothes in layers: they are better than one thick garment","Keep a first aid kit and a blanket in the car","Make sure your mobile phone is fully charged when you go out"],warnings:["Do not place a heater near curtains or clothes","Keep children away from heaters","Do not let heavy snow build up on the roof of the house","Do not overload one electrical outlet with several electric heaters"],activity:{prompt:"You are at home and the gas heater is on. A family member has a headache and feels dizzy. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I get everyone out into fresh air at once (I turn off the heater on the way only if it is quick and safe), call 122 even if they start to feel better, and nobody goes back in until the room is aired and the heater is checked",correct:!0},{text:"I give them a headache tablet and leave the heater on",correct:!1},{text:"I only turn off the heater and we stay in the room",correct:!1},{text:"I do nothing and wait",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"Why should there be an opening for fresh air in the room when a gas heater is on?",options:[{text:"So that the room smells nice",correct:!1},{text:"To prevent the poisonous gas carbon monoxide (CO) from building up",correct:!0},{text:"So that the room does not get cold",correct:!1},{text:"So that no noise is made",correct:!1}]},{question:"Which clothes are best for protection from the cold?",options:[{text:"One thick garment",correct:!1},{text:"Clothes in layers",correct:!0},{text:"Thin clothes",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}]},{question:"How do we prevent water pipes from freezing?",options:[{text:"We wrap the pipes and, on very cold nights, let a little water run slowly",correct:!0},{text:"We do nothing",correct:!1},{text:"We light a fire under the pipes",correct:!1},{text:"We break the pipes",correct:!1}]}]},{id:2,icon:"Sun",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Summer Safety",subtitle:"Protecting yourself from heat and wildfires",summary:"Summer is a hot season with its own dangers, such as heatstroke, crop fires, wildfires, drowning and water shortages. Let us learn how to protect ourselves.",blocks:[{heading:"Protection from Heatstroke",body:"Drink water throughout the day, before you feel thirsty. Stay out of the sun during the hottest hours of the day. Wear light, light-colored clothes and a hat."},{heading:"Heat exhaustion or heatstroke?",body:`• Heat exhaustion: heavy sweating, cold, pale and clammy skin, weakness, dizziness, headache, nausea and thirst. Move the person to a cool place, loosen their clothing, cool them with water and give sips of water if they are fully alert. Get medical help if they vomit, if the symptoms get worse or last more than an hour, or if they become confused (that is heatstroke).
• Heatstroke: a very hot body with confusion, strange behavior, slurred speech, a seizure or collapse; the skin may be hot and dry or still sweating. This is an emergency: call 122, move the person to a cool place, remove extra clothing and cool them at once, before they are taken to the hospital. The best way is to put the body up to the neck in cold water for 15 minutes or until they are thinking clearly again, whichever comes first; if that is not possible, use a cold shower, cold wet towels or sheets and ice packs, and fan them. Give nothing to drink to a person who is confused.`},{heading:"Preventing Wildfires",body:"Do not light fires in forests or among dry grass. Do not throw away cigarettes. Do not leave glass bottles outside, because they can act like a lens, focus the sun's rays and start a fire."},{heading:"Safety at Lakes and Rivers",body:"Never swim alone. Swim only in safe places where there is supervision. Wear a life jacket. Never take your eyes off children."},{heading:"Keeping Food and Water Safe",body:"In summer, food spoils quickly. Keep food in the refrigerator. Drink clean water. Wash fruit and vegetables well."}],tips:["Drink water throughout the day: usually 2 liters or more","Stay out of the sun at midday and in the afternoon, when the heat is at its highest","Use sunscreen (SPF 30+)","Eat fruit with a high water content (such as watermelon and melon)","Never leave children or animals alone in a hot car"],warnings:["Heatstroke can kill: drink plenty of water and stay in the shade","Fire spreads quickly in forests","Drowning: never leave children near water without supervision","Spoiled food causes food poisoning"],activity:{prompt:"You are outside and feel dizzy and weak. Your body has become very hot. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I move into the shade, drink water, cool my body with cool water and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"I carry on with my work",correct:!1},{text:"I stay in the sun and only drink iced water",correct:!1},{text:"I lie down in the sun and go to sleep",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"Why should we not leave glass water bottles outside in the sun?",options:[{text:"Because the water gets hot",correct:!1},{text:"Because they can act like a lens and start a fire",correct:!0},{text:"Because the glass breaks",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}]},{question:"In summer, how much water should we drink?",options:[{text:"Half a liter is enough",correct:!1},{text:"1 liter is enough",correct:!1},{text:"2 liters or more, throughout the day",correct:!0},{text:"Only when we feel thirsty",correct:!1}]},{question:"In summer, at what time of day are the heat and the danger greatest?",options:[{text:"Early morning",correct:!1},{text:"Midday and afternoon",correct:!0},{text:"Evening",correct:!1},{text:"Night",correct:!1}]}]},{id:3,icon:"Home",accent:"#059669",title:"Home Safety",subtitle:"Protecting the family from dangers inside the home",summary:"The home should be a safe place for the family, but there are many dangers in the home, such as fire, electricity, gas, falls and poisoning. Let us learn how to make a home safe.",blocks:[{heading:"Fire Safety and Fire Extinguishers",body:`Install a smoke alarm and test it every month. Keep a fire extinguisher near the kitchen. Types of fire extinguisher (NFPA classes):
• Water: for wood, paper and fabric (Class A).
• CO₂: for electrical equipment (Class C).
• ABC powder: for Classes A, B and C, but not for cooking oil and not for metals.
• Foam: for flammable liquids (Class B).
• Never pour water on burning oil!

Use an extinguisher only if all of this is true: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door. The PASS method: Pull the safety pin, Aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle firmly and Sweep the nozzle from side to side. Plan an emergency escape route from your home.`},{heading:"Electrical Safety and Generators",body:`Keep outlets and electrical cables in good condition. Repair or replace cut or damaged cables. Do not touch electrical appliances with wet hands. Do not connect many appliances to one power strip.

Generators: run a generator only outdoors, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from windows, doors and vents — never inside a house, garage, basement, shop or storeroom, even with the doors and windows open. Fit a battery-powered CO alarm in the home. Never plug a generator into a wall outlet or connect it to the house wiring: it can electrocute neighbors and electricity workers. Only a qualified electrician may connect it, through a transfer switch.`},{heading:"Gas Safety",body:"Check gas pipes and cylinders regularly. If you smell gas: do not switch any electrical switch on or off, do not light any flame, do not use a phone inside, and do not start a generator or a car nearby. Open the windows and doors on your way out if you can. Close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, without going into the area where the gas has collected; if not, do not go near it. Leave the house, warn others and call 115 from outside."},{heading:"Preventing Falls",body:"Fit handrails on stairs. Do not leave things on the floor or on the stairs. Make sure stairs are well lit. Clean up spilled water from the floor immediately."}],tips:["Test the smoke alarm every month","Do not connect many appliances to power strips","Keep medicines and chemicals in a locked place, away from children","Always keep the emergency exit route clear","Write the emergency numbers (115, 122, 104) in a visible place"],warnings:["Do not leave children alone in the kitchen","Keep glass and sharp objects in a high place, away from children","Make windows and balconies safe for children","Playing with fire or electricity can kill"],activity:{prompt:"There is a smell of gas in the kitchen. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I open the windows on my way out, close the gas valve only if I can reach it safely, leave the house and call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"I switch on the light to find where the gas is leaking",correct:!1},{text:"I switch on my mobile phone's flashlight inside the house",correct:!1},{text:"I wait until the smell of gas goes away",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"How often should we test the smoke alarm?",options:[{text:"Once every 5 years",correct:!1},{text:"Every month",correct:!0},{text:"Never",correct:!1},{text:"Only when we buy it",correct:!1}]},{question:"When there is a smell of gas in the house, what do we do?",options:[{text:"We switch on the electric lights",correct:!1},{text:"We open the windows on our way out, close the gas valve only if it is safe to reach, and leave",correct:!0},{text:"We light a cigarette",correct:!1},{text:"We wait until the smell goes away",correct:!1}]},{question:"Why should we not touch electricity with wet hands?",options:[{text:"Because our hands get dirty",correct:!1},{text:"Because water conducts electricity and causes an electric shock",correct:!0},{text:"Because the electricity gets cut off",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}]}]},{id:4,icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#dc2626",title:"First Aid Basics",subtitle:"Basic actions to save a life until the ambulance arrives",summary:"First aid can save a life. If someone is injured, their heart has stopped or they cannot breathe, you can help them until the ambulance arrives. The first thing to do: call 122.",blocks:[{heading:"Chest Compressions (CPR) for Adults",body:`When a person's heart stops (they become unconscious and are not breathing, or not breathing normally):
1. Make sure the area is safe for you.
2. Tap their shoulders and ask in a loud voice: "Are you all right?"
3. If there is no response, call 122 and send someone to bring an automated external defibrillator (AED).
4. Give 30 compressions in the center of the chest: depth 5–6 cm, rate 100–120 compressions per minute.
5. If you are trained, give 2 rescue breaths (tilt the head back, lift the chin and pinch the nose). If you are not trained, or not willing to give breaths, give chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), without stopping.
6. Repeat 30:2 (or compressions only) until the ambulance team arrives.

• AED: if one is available, switch it on immediately and follow the device's voice instructions.`},{heading:"Bleeding: How to Stop It",body:`• Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly. Do not lift your hand until the bleeding stops.
• If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another cloth on top and keep pressing.
• Tourniquet: for very severe bleeding from an arm or leg, apply it 5–7 cm above the wound (not over the knee or elbow) and tighten it until the bleeding stops. Write down the time it was applied and never loosen it.
• If an object is stuck in the wound, do not pull it out! Apply pressure around it and call 122.`},{heading:"Burns: First Aid",body:`• Minor burn: immediately cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes (not ice and not very cold water).
• Do not burst blisters, and do not put butter, oil or toothpaste on the burn.
• After cooling, cover it loosely with a clean cloth or plastic wrap.
• Severe or large burn: call 122. If clothing is stuck to the skin, do not pull it off. Keep the person warm.`},{heading:"Choking",body:`Signs: the person cannot speak, cough or breathe, and holds their throat with their hands.
• Adults and children (one year and older): give 5 blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. Then give 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich): stand behind the person, place your fist slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone, and press quickly inward and upward. Repeat 5+5.
• Infants (under one year): lay the infant along your forearm, face down with the head lower than the chest, and give 5 back blows. Then turn the infant onto their back and give 5 chest thrusts in the center of the chest with the heel of one hand. Never give abdominal thrusts to an infant.
• If the person becomes unconscious, call 122 and start CPR.`}],tips:["Always call 122 as the first step","Use gloves if they are available","Keep a first aid kit at home and in the car","Take a first aid course","Time matters: start helping quickly"],warnings:["Before anything else, check your own safety","If someone has fallen from a height, do not move them (their neck or back may be injured), unless the place is unsafe or they need CPR","Do not put anything in the mouth of an unconscious person","Do not touch a wound with dirty hands"],activity:{prompt:"Someone at home is choking on something and cannot speak or breathe. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I give 5 blows to their back, then 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich), and repeat",correct:!0},{text:"I give them water to drink",correct:!1},{text:"I push my fingers deep into their mouth",correct:!1},{text:"I wait until they feel better",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"In adult CPR, what is the ratio of compressions to breaths?",options:[{text:"10 compressions and 1 breath",correct:!1},{text:"30 compressions and 2 breaths",correct:!0},{text:"15 compressions and 2 breaths",correct:!1},{text:"50 compressions and 5 breaths",correct:!1}]},{question:"What do we do for a minor burn?",options:[{text:"We put ice on it",correct:!1},{text:"We put butter on it",correct:!1},{text:"Cool running water for 20 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"We put kerosene on it",correct:!1}]},{question:"What is the ambulance number?",options:[{text:"115",correct:!1},{text:"122",correct:!0},{text:"104",correct:!1},{text:"911",correct:!1}]}]},{id:5,icon:"Backpack",accent:"#9333ea",title:"The Emergency Kit",subtitle:"Preparing a bag for emergencies",summary:"An emergency kit is a bag that holds the things you need in an emergency, such as an earthquake, a flood or any other incident. Every household should have one.",blocks:[{heading:"Water and Food",body:`• Water: about 4 liters per person per day, for at least 3 days (for drinking and hygiene).
• Canned food or dry food that does not need cooking.
• Crackers, dates and candy.`},{heading:"Medical Supplies",body:"Bandages, clean wound dressings, medical tape, gloves, scissors, tweezers, and any medicines that family members need (if any)."},{heading:"Important Equipment",body:"A flashlight and batteries, a small radio, a power bank to charge your mobile phone, a change of clothes, a blanket and a whistle to call for help."},{heading:"Documents and Money",body:"Copies of identity cards and passports, some cash, a list of important phone numbers and spare keys."}],tips:["Choose a light bag that is easy to carry","Check the contents of the kit once every 6 months and replace any water or food that has expired","Keep the kit in an easy-to-reach place near the door","Include items for the particular needs of each family member (children, the elderly, the sick)","Do not forget your pets"],warnings:["Make sure there is enough water and food for every family member","Do not forget your medicines","Include a mobile phone and a charger","Cash is important, because bank cards may not work"],activity:{prompt:"After an earthquake, you must evacuate your home quickly. The emergency kit is ready at home. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I take the kit and go to the assembly point",correct:!0},{text:"I spend time looking around to collect other things",correct:!1},{text:"I leave the kit behind and just run",correct:!1},{text:"I stay at home and wait",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"How much water should the emergency kit hold for each person?",options:[{text:"1 liter is enough",correct:!1},{text:"About 4 liters per day, for at least 3 days",correct:!0},{text:"Half a liter",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}]},{question:"How often should we check the contents of the kit?",options:[{text:"Once every 10 years",correct:!1},{text:"Once every 6 months",correct:!0},{text:"Only when we buy it",correct:!1},{text:"It never needs checking",correct:!1}]},{question:"Which of these items is NOT needed in an emergency kit?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"A flashlight",correct:!1},{text:"A television",correct:!0},{text:"Medicines",correct:!1}]}]},{id:6,icon:"PhoneCall",accent:"#16a34a",title:"Emergency Numbers",subtitle:"Knowing the numbers and how to call",summary:"Knowing the emergency numbers can save a life. Everyone should memorize these numbers and know when and how to call.",blocks:[{heading:"The Main Numbers in the Kurdistan Region",body:`• 115: Civil Defense (fire, rescue, building collapse, floods, gas leaks and hazardous materials).
• 122: Ambulance (medical emergencies).
• 104: Police.`},{heading:"How to Make the Call",body:"Stay calm and speak clearly. Say the exact location first: the area, the street, a nearby landmark and the floor. Then say what has happened, how many people are hurt or trapped, and any danger (fire spreading, gas, electricity, chemicals). Give your name and your phone number. Answer the questions, follow their instructions and do not hang up until they tell you to."},{heading:"Which Number to Call, and When",body:`• Fire, smoke, explosion, collapse, flood or a trapped person: 115.
• Injury, serious illness or an unconscious person: 122.
• Theft, threats and security incidents: 104.
• If there is a fire or incident and also injured people: 115 and 122.`},{heading:"The Exact Address",body:"Give the name of the neighborhood and the street, visible landmarks nearby (a mosque, a school, a shop), and the house number and its color."}],tips:["Save the numbers in your mobile phone","Put a list of the numbers in a visible place at home","Teach the numbers to children","Teach children the home address","Memorize your home address exactly"],warnings:["Never make false or prank calls: they keep help away from people who need it","Do not keep the emergency line busy without a reason: other people's lives are at risk","Stay calm and speak clearly","The exact address is very important"],activity:{prompt:"You saw a car hit a person on the street, and the person was injured. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I call 122, give the address and report the injured person's condition",correct:!0},{text:"I continue on my way",correct:!1},{text:"I record videos",correct:!1},{text:"I just watch",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"What is the Civil Defense number (fire and rescue)?",options:[{text:"122",correct:!1},{text:"104",correct:!1},{text:"115",correct:!0},{text:"911",correct:!1}]},{question:"When you call, what information should you give?",options:[{text:"Only your name",correct:!1},{text:"The exact location first, then what has happened, how many people are hurt or trapped, any danger, and your name and phone number",correct:!0},{text:"Only the address",correct:!1},{text:"No information is needed",correct:!1}]},{question:"What is the ambulance number?",options:[{text:"115",correct:!1},{text:"122",correct:!0},{text:"104",correct:!1},{text:"100",correct:!1}]}]},{id:7,icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire Extinguishers: Complete Training",subtitle:"Types of extinguisher, fire classes and the PASS method",summary:"A fire extinguisher is a key piece of equipment for putting out small fires in their early stages. Every adult, at home and at work, should know how to use one quickly and correctly, because portable extinguishers only work for a few seconds. If the fire becomes large, leave the extinguisher, get out and call 115.",blocks:[{heading:"Fire Classes (NFPA)",body:`• Class A: ordinary solid materials (wood, paper, fabric).
• Class B: flammable liquids and gases (gasoline, kerosene, gas). Water spreads a burning liquid.
• Class C: live electrical equipment. Do not use water or foam.
• Class D: combustible metals (such as magnesium). Water causes a violent reaction and an explosion.
• Class K: cooking oils and fats. Water spreads hot oil into a large fire.`},{heading:"Types of Fire Extinguisher",body:`Classes are given in NFPA letters:
• Water: Class A only.
• CO₂: Class B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires). It has a short range and is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires. Hold only the handle and do not touch the nozzle: it becomes extremely cold and burns the skin. In a small room, leave and ventilate after use, because CO₂ pushes out the oxygen.
• ABC powder: for A, B and C. Not for Class D and not for Class K.
• Foam: for A and B. Do not use it on electrical equipment.
• Clean agent: for sensitive equipment such as computers; its rating depends on the model, so read the label.
• Wet chemical: for cooking oil (Class K).
• Class D: only an extinguisher or agent whose label lists the burning metal.`},{heading:"The PASS Method",body:`Before you use an extinguisher (every answer must be yes):
• The fire is small and just starting, like a fire in a wastebasket or a pan.
• The extinguisher is the right type for this fire.
• Your exit is behind you and the fire is not between you and the exit.
• The alarm has been raised: others are warned and 115 has been called.
• You are trained to use the extinguisher.
• No smoke or heat is blocking your way out.
• If the fire does not go out at once, stop, get out and close the door behind you.

1. P = Pull: pull out the safety pin.
2. A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top of the flames.
3. S = Squeeze: squeeze the extinguisher handle firmly.
4. S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side until the fire is out.

PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions.`},{heading:"Checking the Extinguisher",body:`Visual check every month, by the person in charge:
• The extinguisher is in its place, visible and easy to reach.
• The safety pin and its seal are in place.
• The label is readable and faces out.
• The hose is not cracked or punctured, and the nozzle is clear.
• The body of the extinguisher is not rusted, damaged or leaking.
• If there is a pressure gauge, its needle is in the green zone. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.

Service by a qualified technician:
• Inspection and maintenance: once a year.
• Recharge: after any use, even a short one.
• 6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years.
• Hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder): every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells.

A test date is not a replacement date. An extinguisher is taken out of service if it fails an inspection or test, is damaged, obsolete or recalled, has no readable label or is a non-rechargeable unit past the service life on its label. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed.`},{heading:"Particular Risks at Home and in the Community",body:`• Kitchen: cooking oil is Class K. Use a wet chemical extinguisher or a large lid.
• Electricity: outlets and cables are Class C.
• Gas heaters and cylinders: gas is Class B. The priority is to stop the gas: a qualified person closes the gas valve only if it is safe to reach. Do not put out a gas flame if the gas cannot be stopped safely: get everyone out and call 115.
• Gasoline and kerosene: store them away from the house in a well-ventilated place.
• An ABC extinguisher is good for most home fires, but not for cooking oil (K) and not for metals (D).`},{heading:"Extinguisher Colors (BS EN 3 and BS 7863)",body:`Color is not the fire class. Under the European standard BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent:
• Cream = foam.
• Blue = dry powder.
• Black = CO₂.
• Yellow = wet chemical.
• Water has no band.

Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols.`},{heading:"Class F (Cooking Oil): the Same as Class K",body:`Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. So Class F (EN 2) and Class K (NFPA) are the same class: fires in cooking oils and fats.
• Very hot oil can catch fire by itself.
• A wet chemical extinguisher is the right choice: it forms a soap-like layer on the oil that cuts off the oxygen and cools the oil.
• Restaurants and large kitchens need a Class F/K extinguisher.`}],tips:["Every home should have at least one ABC extinguisher","Place the extinguisher near the kitchen door, not inside the kitchen next to the stove","Teach all adult family members how to use the extinguisher","Check the extinguisher every month; if it has a pressure gauge, the needle must be in the green zone (CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full)","Do not pull the safety pin until you need to use the extinguisher","Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller","Keep your back toward the exit door, so that your escape route always stays open","Once a year, have the extinguisher serviced by a qualified person (Service)","A portable extinguisher only works for a few seconds: be ready before you start"],warnings:["Never pour water on burning oil: a large fire will spread quickly!","Never use water on electrical equipment: risk of electric shock!","Do not use the extinguisher if the fire is bigger than a wastebasket, is spreading or smoke is building up: get out and call 115!","Always aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top!","CO₂ extinguisher: do not touch the nozzle! It becomes as cold as about 78 degrees below zero and burns the skin.","ABC powder in a closed room reduces visibility and makes breathing difficult: after using it, leave the room and open the doors and windows"],activity:{prompt:"In the kitchen at home, the oil in a frying pan on the stove has caught fire! What will you do?",options:[{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1},{text:"If it is safe, I turn off the stove and put a large lid on the pan to cut off the oxygen, or I use a wet chemical extinguisher",correct:!0},{text:"I pick up the pan and carry it outside",correct:!1},{text:"I beat it with a cloth to put it out",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"In NFPA letters, which fire classes is a CO₂ extinguisher rated for?",options:[{text:"A and D",correct:!1},{text:"B and C",correct:!0},{text:"A and K",correct:!1},{text:"D and K",correct:!1}]},{question:"In the PASS method, where should the nozzle be aimed?",options:[{text:"At the top of the fire",correct:!1},{text:"At the base of the fire",correct:!0},{text:"Around the fire",correct:!1},{text:"At the ceiling of the room",correct:!1}]},{question:"Why is water not used on an oil fire (Class K)?",options:[{text:"Because it has no effect",correct:!1},{text:"Because the water instantly turns to steam and spreads the hot oil into a large fire",correct:!0},{text:"Because the oil absorbs the water",correct:!1},{text:"Because water itself burns",correct:!1}]},{question:"Which extinguisher is suitable for most home fires (Classes A, B and C)?",options:[{text:"Water extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"CO₂ extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"ABC powder extinguisher",correct:!0},{text:"Foam extinguisher",correct:!1}]},{question:"In the UK color code (BS EN 3 with BS 7863), what color is the band on a wet chemical extinguisher (for cooking oil)?",options:[{text:"Red",correct:!1},{text:"Black",correct:!1},{text:"Yellow",correct:!0},{text:"Blue",correct:!1}]},{question:"Under NFPA 10, how often should a qualified person carry out maintenance on an extinguisher?",options:[{text:"Every week",correct:!1},{text:"Once a year, with a visual check every month",correct:!0},{text:"Once every 20 years",correct:!1},{text:"It does not need checking",correct:!1}]}]},{id:8,icon:"Heart",accent:"#db2777",title:"First Aid and Chest Compressions (CPR)",subtitle:"Complete training in first aid and cardiopulmonary resuscitation",summary:"First aid is the initial help given to an injured or sick person before the medical team arrives. Everyone in the community should know how to perform CPR, stop bleeding and protect an injured person until the ambulance arrives. This lesson can help protect your life and the lives of your family.",blocks:[{heading:"CPR: Reviving the Heart and Lungs",body:`1. Check that the area is safe.
2. Tap the casualty's shoulders and ask: "Are you all right?"
3. If they do not respond and are not breathing normally, call 122 and ask for an automated external defibrillator (AED).
4. Give 30 compressions in the center of the chest: 100–120 compressions per minute, depth 5–6 cm for all adults.
5. If you are trained and willing, give 2 breaths, each lasting one second. If you are not trained, or not willing to give breaths, give chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), without stopping.
6. Continue 30:2 (or compressions only) until the ambulance team arrives.
7. If an AED is available, use it immediately.`},{heading:"Stopping Bleeding",body:`• Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly.
• If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another cloth on top and keep pressing.
• Tourniquet: for very severe bleeding from an arm or leg, apply it 5–7 cm above the wound, write down the time it was applied and never loosen it.
• Do not pull out an object that is stuck in a wound!`},{heading:"Treating Burns",body:`• Heat burns: cool running water for 20 minutes.
• Chemical burns: rinse with plenty of water for at least 20 minutes.
• Electrical burns: first cut off the electricity! Then call 122.
• Do not burst blisters, and do not use butter, oil or toothpaste.`},{heading:"Fractures and Falls",body:`• Do not move the injured limb: keep it still with a stick or a board (splint).
• Do not move a person who has fallen from a height: their neck or back may be injured. Keep them still and call 122. Move them only if the place is unsafe (fire, traffic, collapse, gas, rising water), keeping the head, neck and back in line. If they need CPR, lay them on their back on a firm surface. Do not put them in the recovery position.
• Wrap ice in a cloth and place it on the swelling.`},{heading:"Electric Shock",body:`• Cut off the electricity immediately! Do not touch the casualty until the power has been cut off.
• If you cannot cut off the power (household electricity only), use a dry piece of wood to move the cable away from the casualty.
• Call 122; anyone who has had an electric shock must be examined by a doctor.`},{heading:"Choking: the Heimlich Maneuver",body:`If a person cannot speak, cough or breathe and is holding their throat with their hands, they are choking on something.
• Give 5 blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades.
• Give 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich): stand behind the person, place your fist slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone, grasp it with your other hand and press quickly inward and upward.
• Continue 5+5 until the object comes out or the person becomes unconscious; if they become unconscious, call 122 and start CPR.
• Infants (under one year): 5 back blows (head lower than the chest) and 5 chest thrusts. Never give abdominal thrusts to an infant.`},{heading:"The Recovery Position",body:`If a person is unconscious but breathing normally, and no neck or back injury is suspected, place them in the recovery position:
1. Place their arm nearest to you at a right angle, pointing toward their head.
2. Bring their far arm across their chest and place the back of that hand under their cheek.
3. Raise the knee of their far leg.
4. Holding the knee and the shoulder, roll them toward you onto their side.
5. Tilt their head back slightly so that the airway stays open.

• Call 122 and watch their breathing until the ambulance arrives.`}],tips:["Every home should have a first aid kit","Keep the list of emergency numbers in a visible place","Teach the whole family CPR and the Heimlich maneuver","Keep clean cloths, a tourniquet and scissors in the kit","122 is always available: do not be afraid to call","The brain is quickly damaged without oxygen: start CPR quickly","For all adults, including the elderly, the compression depth is 5–6 centimeters: do not reduce it","For a pregnant woman or a very overweight person who is choking, give thrusts in the center of the chest, not on the abdomen"],warnings:["Do not touch the casualty if the electricity has not been cut off!","Do not pull out an object that is stuck in a wound!","Do not burst burn blisters!","Do not move a person who has fallen from a height, unless the place is unsafe or they need CPR!","Do not use abdominal thrusts (Heimlich) on infants under one year old!","Continue CPR until the ambulance team arrives: do not stop"],activity:{prompt:"A friend's hand caught fire and they have a large burn. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I put butter or oil on it",correct:!1},{text:"I cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"I burst the blisters",correct:!1},{text:"I do nothing",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"In CPR, how many chest compressions should be given per minute?",options:[{text:"50–70",correct:!1},{text:"80–90",correct:!1},{text:"100–120",correct:!0},{text:"150 or more",correct:!1}]},{question:"If a sharp object is stuck in a wound, what will you do?",options:[{text:"I pull it out",correct:!1},{text:"I leave it in place and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"I leave the person and go on my way",correct:!1},{text:"I wash it with water and pull it out",correct:!1}]},{question:"If someone gets an electric shock, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"I take hold of their hand",correct:!1},{text:"I start CPR immediately",correct:!1},{text:"I cut off the electricity",correct:!0},{text:"I pour water on them",correct:!1}]},{question:"What is the main sign of complete choking?",options:[{text:"A loud noise from the throat",correct:!1},{text:"Being unable to speak, cough or breathe, with the hands at the throat",correct:!0},{text:"A mild cough",correct:!1},{text:"A headache",correct:!1}]},{question:"Who is the recovery position used for?",options:[{text:"An unconscious person who is still breathing normally",correct:!0},{text:"An unconscious person who is not breathing",correct:!1},{text:"A conscious person with a minor wound",correct:!1},{text:"A healthy person with no problems",correct:!1}]}]},{id:9,icon:"CarFront",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Road Safety",subtitle:"Protecting yourself and your family on the roads",summary:"Road traffic accidents are one of the biggest causes of death and injury. Learning the traffic laws and following them can protect your life. This lesson covers the safety of pedestrians, drivers and children.",blocks:[{heading:"Pedestrian Safety",body:"Always walk on the sidewalk. Before crossing the road, look both ways and cross at a pedestrian crossing. Do not look at your mobile phone while crossing. At night, wear light-colored clothes or clothes with reflective markings."},{heading:"Driving Safety",body:"Always wear your seat belt. Do not use your mobile phone while driving. Keep to a suitable, legal speed. In winter, use suitable tires."},{heading:"Children in the Car",body:"Always put children in a child car seat that suits their age and weight. Children aged 12 and under should sit in the back seats. Never leave children alone in the car."},{heading:"Motorcycles and Bicycles",body:"Always wear a protective helmet. At night, lights and reflective markings are essential. Wear gloves and suitable footwear."}],tips:["Always wear your seat belt","Do not use your mobile phone while driving","Children should sit in the back seats","Wear a protective helmet on motorcycles and bicycles"],warnings:["Mobile phone + driving = danger of death","Speed + snow or ice = accident"],activity:{prompt:"You are driving and your mobile phone rings. An important message has arrived. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I keep driving and read the message",correct:!1},{text:"I stop in a safe place at the side of the road and then reply",correct:!0},{text:"I use the phone with one hand",correct:!1},{text:"I type a quick reply while driving",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"Up to what age should children sit in the back seats of a car?",options:[{text:"12 years",correct:!0},{text:"6 years",correct:!1},{text:"18 years",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}]},{question:"Which of these is one of the most dangerous things to do while driving?",options:[{text:"Using a mobile phone",correct:!0},{text:"Wearing a seat belt",correct:!1},{text:"Driving at the legal speed",correct:!1},{text:"Stopping at a red traffic light",correct:!1}]}]},{id:10,icon:"LifeBuoy",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Water Safety and Drowning Prevention",subtitle:"Protecting yourself and children near water",summary:"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), drowning is one of the leading causes of death among children and young people worldwide. In the Kurdistan Region, lakes, dams and rivers are very dangerous in summer. Every family needs to learn about water safety.",blocks:[{heading:"Children and Water",body:"Never leave children alone near water: a small child can drown in very shallow water (a few centimeters, such as in a bucket or a basin). Near pools and water, always keep your eyes on them."},{heading:"Swimming Safely",body:"Swim only in official places where there is supervision. Always have someone else with you. Before going into the water, find out how deep it is. Wear a life jacket at lakes and rivers."},{heading:"Rescue",body:`If someone is drowning:
1. Do not go into the water yourself!
2. Reach out or throw something to them (a rope, a stick, a life ring).
3. Call 115 and 122.
4. Once they are out of the water, check their breathing. If they are not breathing normally and you are trained, start CPR with breaths: drowning stops the breathing first, so breaths matter most. If you are not trained or cannot give breaths, give chest compressions only until help arrives.
5. Anyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor, even if they seem well: lung problems are common afterwards.`}],tips:["Do not leave children alone near water","Wear a life jacket at lakes and rivers","Do not go into the water to rescue someone: throw something to them","Learn CPR"],warnings:["A few centimeters of water are enough to drown a small child","The water in the region's rivers and dams can be very strong and cold, even if it looks calm on the surface"],activity:{prompt:"You and your family are near a lake. Your small child wants to go into the water. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I let them go: it is normal",correct:!1},{text:"I put a life jacket on them, go in with them and watch them closely",correct:!0},{text:"I tell them to stay at the edge and I move away",correct:!1},{text:"I watch from a distance",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"If someone is drowning, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"I go into the water to rescue them",correct:!1},{text:"I throw something to them (a rope or a stick) and call 115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"I just shout",correct:!1},{text:"I do nothing",correct:!1}]}]},{id:11,icon:"Activity",accent:"#d97706",title:"Earthquakes at Home and in the Community",subtitle:"Preparedness and response",summary:"Earthquakes come without warning. Family preparedness and knowing the correct actions during an earthquake save lives. Our area is not free from earthquake risk, so every family should be prepared.",blocks:[{heading:"During an Earthquake",body:`1. Drop, Cover and Hold On: get under a sturdy table and hold on to its leg.
2. Stay away from windows, glass and heavy objects.
3. Do not run outside, do not stand in a doorway and do not use the elevator.
4. If you are outside: move away from buildings, trees and power lines.`},{heading:"The Emergency Kit",body:"Every family should prepare an emergency kit: water (about 4 liters per person per day, for at least 3 days), food that does not spoil, medicines, a battery-powered radio, a flashlight, blankets and important documents."},{heading:"After an Earthquake",body:"If you smell gas or hear gas leaking: do not touch any switch or flame, open the windows on your way out, close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, then get out and call 115 from outside. Do not go into damaged buildings. Expect aftershocks. If people are trapped under buildings or there are injured people, call 115 and 122."}],tips:["Drop, Cover and Hold On","Always keep your emergency kit ready","Shut off the gas only if you smell gas or hear a leak, and only if the valve is safe to reach","Do not stay in damaged buildings"],warnings:["Do not run while the ground is shaking!","Do not go into damaged buildings!"],activity:{prompt:"A strong earthquake strikes. You are at home and the children are frightened. What will you do?",options:[{text:"We get under a sturdy table together with the children, cover our heads and hold on to the table leg",correct:!0},{text:"We run outside",correct:!1},{text:"We stand by the window",correct:!1},{text:"We take the elevator down",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"During an earthquake at home, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Get under a sturdy table and cover your head",correct:!0},{text:"Run outside",correct:!1},{text:"Stand by the window",correct:!1},{text:"Use the elevator",correct:!1}]}]},{id:12,icon:"Siren",accent:"#dc2626",title:"The Work of Civil Defense",subtitle:"The duties of the 115 teams and how you can help them",summary:"Civil Defense is an important government agency that protects the lives of citizens and private and public property. In the Kurdistan Region, 115 is the Civil Defense number for fire, rescue and disasters, and its teams are ready day and night. Citizens should know how to call 115 and how to help the teams, not hinder them.",blocks:[{heading:"The Main Duties of Civil Defense",body:`• Firefighting: in homes, offices, factories, farmland and forests.
• Rescue: from vehicles in accidents, from under rubble and from water.
• Floods and disasters: evacuating people, pumping out water and helping those affected.
• Hazardous materials (HazMat): gas leaks and chemicals.
• Training and awareness: lessons in schools and government offices.
• Safety inspections: in markets, restaurants, halls and high-rise buildings.`},{heading:"Official Structure",body:`• In the Kurdistan Region, Civil Defense comes under the Ministry of Interior.
• Each governorate has a Civil Defense Directorate, such as the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate, with stations in the city and the districts.
• Departments: firefighting, rescue, first aid, training and technical departments.
• In major disasters, Civil Defense works together with the ambulance service (122), the police (104) and other agencies.`},{heading:"Their Equipment and Vehicles",body:`• Fire engines: carry water, hoses and pumps.
• Ladder trucks: reach high floors.
• Rescue equipment: hydraulic tools for cutting through the metal of vehicles and freeing trapped people (Jaws of Life).
• Breathing apparatus (BA): gives the firefighter clean air inside smoke.
• Special clothing for protection from heat.
• Radios for communication between teams.`},{heading:"How to Call 115",body:`1. Dial 115.
2. Stay calm and speak clearly.
3. Say: the exact address (neighborhood, street and a visible landmark nearby), what has happened (a fire? an accident? is someone trapped?), how many people are injured or trapped, any particular hazard (gas, chemicals, electricity), and your name and phone number so that they can call you back.
4. Do not hang up until they tell you to.
5. If it is safe, keep the road clear for the fire engines.
6. Send someone out to the street to show the team the way.`},{heading:"How the Community Helps 115",body:`• On the road: when you hear a siren, calmly move to the right and stop so that the road is clear.
• Do not make false calls: every false call takes a team away from a real emergency.
• Keep access routes clear: do not park cars in front of gates or in narrow alleys.
• Witnesses: if you saw the incident, stay and give your information to the team.
• Do not crowd around the team or take photos, so that you do not get in their way.
• If the team asks for help, help them.
• If you see that 115 is needed, do not delay: call.
• Stay calm and do not spread fear and confusion.`},{heading:"Working and Volunteering with Civil Defense",body:`• Applications for employment are made through official Civil Defense announcements.
• Requirements for age, education, physical fitness and tests are set by the Directorate: for up-to-date information, contact the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate.
• New staff receive basic training in firefighting, rescue and first aid.
• Citizens can take part in awareness lessons and training and become helpers in their own neighborhood during emergencies.`}],tips:["Memorize 115, and make sure every family member does too","Know your home address exactly (neighborhood, street, house number)","When you hear a siren, move to the right and stop, not in the middle of the road","Use 115 only for emergencies, so that the line stays open for people who need it","If you suspect a fire or another danger, call: it is better for the team to come and find no problem","Display the house number and the neighborhood name on your door so that the team can reach you easily","Write a home escape plan and practice it with your family"],warnings:["Do not make false calls to 115: they waste the teams' time and put lives at risk","Do not crowd around 115 vehicles or take photos: you get in the way of their work","Do not film or share videos of victims: respect them","When you hear a siren, do not drive behind emergency vehicles, and keep the road clear for them"],activity:{prompt:"A car accident has happened in front of your house. 2 people are trapped inside the car and no one else is there. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I immediately call 115 and 122 and give the exact address. If there is no fire, I do not pull out the trapped casualties, so that their neck and back are not injured. I stay in a safe place and follow the instructions of 115.",correct:!0},{text:"I quickly try to pull out the trapped casualties",correct:!1},{text:"I record a video and post it on social media",correct:!1},{text:"I go to find someone else to help and do not call",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"In the Kurdistan Region, what is the Civil Defense emergency number?",options:[{text:"991",correct:!1},{text:"107",correct:!1},{text:"115",correct:!0},{text:"122",correct:!1}]},{question:"When you see a fire engine with its siren on, what do you do?",options:[{text:"I calmly move to the right and stop so that the road is clear",correct:!0},{text:"I drive quickly behind it",correct:!1},{text:"I block its way",correct:!1},{text:"I record videos",correct:!1}]},{question:"When you call 115, what is the most important thing to say first?",options:[{text:"Only your name",correct:!1},{text:"The address and what has happened",correct:!0},{text:'I say "Come quickly" and hang up',correct:!1},{text:"I ask when they will arrive",correct:!1}]},{question:"What does a BA (Breathing Apparatus) do?",options:[{text:"It is a fire extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"It is a breathing device that gives the firefighter clean air inside smoke",correct:!0},{text:"It is a radio",correct:!1},{text:"It is a siren",correct:!1}]},{question:"If there is a fire and also injured people, which number do you call?",options:[{text:"Only 104",correct:!1},{text:"Only 122",correct:!1},{text:"115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"I do not call and I wait",correct:!1}]}]},{id:13,icon:"FlameKindling",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Types of Fire",subtitle:"Not all fires are the same: each type needs its own method",summary:"Under the American system (NFPA 10), there are five classes of fire: A, B, C, D and K. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. Each class involves a different burning material and needs a suitable extinguisher. Choosing the wrong extinguisher can make the fire bigger. This lesson will teach you how to recognize the type of fire and what to use.",blocks:[{heading:"Class A: Solid Materials (Wood, Paper, Fabric)",body:`Very common at home: paper, cardboard, curtains, fabric, plastic and wooden furniture. Extinguisher: water or ABC powder. Aim at the base of the fire, starting from the distance shown on the extinguisher label, with your exit behind you.
• If your clothes catch fire: Stop, Drop and Roll.`},{heading:"Class B: Flammable Liquids and Gases (Gasoline, Kerosene, Alcohol)",body:`These liquids catch fire quickly, and their vapors can ignite from a distance. Do not use water: water spreads the burning liquid and makes the fire bigger. Extinguisher for liquids: foam, CO₂ or ABC powder. Spread the foam gently over the surface of the liquid so that it covers it.
• Alcohol, acetone and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC powder or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam; ordinary foam breaks down on them.
• Gas fire (LPG, natural gas): the priority is to stop the gas. A qualified person closes the gas valve only if it is safe to reach. Do not put out a gas flame if the gas cannot be stopped safely: unburned gas can build up and explode. If it is not safe, get everyone out, raise the alarm and call 115. Foam is not the general choice for gas fires. Under EN 2, gas fires are Class C.
• BLEVE: if a fire heats a gas cylinder or a tank, it can burst in a large explosion. Move away from it and call 115.`},{heading:"Class C: Electrical Equipment (NFPA)",body:`Computers, televisions, refrigerators, outlets and electrical cables.
• Never use water! An electric shock can kill.
• First cut off the electricity (pull out the plug or switch off the main power supply to the house), then use CO₂, a clean agent or ABC powder.
• ABC powder damages electronic equipment; CO₂ is better for sensitive equipment.
• In the European system (EN 2), electrical fire is not a separate class.`},{heading:"Class D: Combustible Metals (Magnesium, Titanium, Sodium, Lithium Metal)",body:`These metals burn at very high temperatures.
• Do not use water or CO₂ on burning metals: they cause a violent reaction and a bigger fire.
• Use only a Class D extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; the agent differs from metal to metal (lithium, for example, needs an agent listed for lithium). Never use water, ordinary powder or foam. Dry sand only if the site fire plan or the metal's safety data sheet (SDS) names it and it is completely dry. Call 115.
• The lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries in mobile phones, laptops, power banks and electric bicycles contain no metallic lithium and are not Class D: if they catch fire, cool them with plenty of water and call 115. Class D is only for lithium-metal batteries.`},{heading:"Class K (NFPA) / F (EN 2): Cooking Oil",body:`A fire in hot oil in a frying pan or a pot on the stove.
• Never use water! Water turns to steam instantly inside hot oil and spreads the burning oil in an explosion.
• ABC powder is not suitable for this class.
• Use a wet chemical extinguisher, or turn off the stove and put a large lid or a fire blanket over the pan to cut off the oxygen.
• Do not pick up the pan or carry it outside.`},{heading:"Stages of Fire Development",body:`1. Incipient: the fire is small and there is little smoke. Only at this stage is it safe to use an extinguisher.
2. Growth: the heat increases and the fire spreads.
3. Fully developed: the whole room is on fire.
4. Decay: the fire dies down, but if air suddenly enters the room, there is a risk of a fire explosion (backdraft).

• Flashover: when the hot smoke layer under the ceiling reaches about 500–600 degrees Celsius, everything in the room catches fire at the same time.`}],tips:["Before buying, read the fire classes written on the extinguisher label","Keep at least one ABC extinguisher at home, and a wet chemical extinguisher or a fire blanket for the kitchen","Under NFPA 10, in workplaces the travel distance to a Class A extinguisher must not exceed 23 meters","According to NFPA, cooking is the leading cause of home fires: do not leave the kitchen while cooking","Electrical equipment fires: never use water. The first step is to cut off the electricity","Lithium-ion batteries: if one gets hot, swells or gives off smoke, put it away from flammable materials, move away from it and call 115","Cooking oil: turn off the stove and put a large lid over the pan. This is the first step in putting out that kind of fire"],warnings:["Water on burning oil = steam explosion and a large fire","Water on electrical equipment = fatal shock","ABC powder on a computer = damage to the equipment","Cool a burning lithium-ion battery with plenty of water and call 115: the fire can flare up again","A fire bigger than a trash can = get out immediately, do not use an extinguisher"],activity:{prompt:"You are in the kitchen. The oil in a frying pan has caught fire and the stove is still on. Your family is in the bedroom. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I turn off the stove, put a large lid on the pan and warn my family; if the fire does not go out, we leave and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"I pour water on it to cool it down",correct:!1},{text:"I pick up the pan and carry it outside",correct:!1},{text:"I run out alone and do not warn my family",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"A computer in your room has caught fire. Which method is correct?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"I first cut off the electricity, then use CO₂, a clean agent or ABC powder",correct:!0},{text:"Foam (AFFF Foam)",correct:!1},{text:"Wet chemical",correct:!1}]},{question:"The oil in a frying pan on a gas stove has caught fire. What is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1},{text:"I close the gas valve and put a large lid on the pan",correct:!0},{text:"I pour ABC powder on it",correct:!1},{text:"I beat it with a cloth",correct:!1}]},{question:"The lithium-ion battery of a mobile phone has caught fire. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I cover it with a blanket and leave it in the room",correct:!1},{text:"I just pour ABC powder on it and leave",correct:!1},{text:"If it is safe, I move it away from flammable materials, cool it with plenty of water and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"I just spray CO₂ on it and leave it",correct:!1}]},{question:"Flashover happens when the hot smoke layer under the ceiling reaches about what temperature?",options:[{text:"200 degrees Celsius",correct:!1},{text:"300 degrees Celsius",correct:!1},{text:"500–600 degrees Celsius",correct:!0},{text:"1000 degrees Celsius",correct:!1}]},{question:"According to NFPA, which activity is the leading cause of home fires?",options:[{text:"Reading books",correct:!1},{text:"Washing clothes by hand",correct:!1},{text:"Cooking (the kitchen)",correct:!0},{text:"Sleeping",correct:!1}]}]},{id:14,icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#0891b2",title:"How to Use a Fire Extinguisher",subtitle:"Types of extinguisher, the PASS method, and when to leave the extinguisher and get out",summary:"Fire extinguishers are found in homes, offices and public places, but many people do not know how to use them. A portable extinguisher only works for a few seconds, so you must be prepared before a fire starts. This lesson teaches you the PASS method, how to choose the right extinguisher and, most important of all, when you should not use the extinguisher and should get out.",blocks:[{heading:"Main Types of Extinguisher",body:`Classes in NFPA letters:
• Water: Class A only.
• Foam: A and B.
• CO₂: Class B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires). Short range; not for deep-seated wood or paper fires; in a small room, leave and ventilate after use.
• ABC powder: A, B and C, the most common type; not for D and not for K.
• Wet chemical: K, cooking oil.
• Clean agent: for sensitive electronic equipment; its rating depends on the model, so read the label.

Color is not the fire class. Under BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent: cream = foam, blue = dry powder, black = CO₂, yellow = wet chemical; water has no band. Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols.

Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F.

For the home: an ABC extinguisher, plus a wet chemical extinguisher or a fire blanket for the kitchen.`},{heading:"PASS: Step by Step",body:`Before you start, every answer must be yes: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out.

1. P = Pull: pull out the safety pin.
2. A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top. Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.
3. S = Squeeze: squeeze the handle firmly and steadily.
4. S = Sweep: sweep slowly from side to side until the fire is out.

• If the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door.
• Small extinguishers empty quickly, often in a few seconds; the exact discharge time is on the label.
• PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions.`},{heading:"Where to Stand: Safety Comes First",body:`Always keep your back toward the door (your exit route). Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller. If the heat becomes too great, move back. Never let the fire get between you and the door.
• Smoke and heat can kill quickly: if the room fills with smoke, get out immediately.`},{heading:"When Not to Use an Extinguisher",body:`• The fire is bigger than a trash can or is spreading quickly.
• The room is filling with smoke.
• You do not know what material is burning.
• Your exit route is not behind you.
• No one has called 115 yet (call first).
• The fire is getting close to a gas cylinder or a liquid fuel tank.
• Flammable liquids have spread over a large area.

In all of these situations: first get out, then call 115.`},{heading:"Checking the Extinguisher at Home",body:`Every month (visual check, by the person in charge):
1. The extinguisher is in its place and visible, and the way to it is clear.
2. The safety pin and its seal are intact.
3. The body of the extinguisher is clean, free of rust, undamaged and not leaking; the hose and nozzle are clear.
4. The instruction label is easy to read and faces out.
5. If there is a pressure gauge, its needle is in the green zone. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.

Service by a qualified technician:
• Inspection and maintenance: once a year, recorded on the extinguisher's tag.
• Recharge: after any use, even a short one.
• 6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years.
• Hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder): every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells.

A test date is not a replacement date. An extinguisher is taken out of service if it fails an inspection or test, is damaged, obsolete or recalled, has no readable label or is a non-rechargeable unit past the service life on its label. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed.`},{heading:"Where Should Extinguishers Be Placed?",body:`• At home (at least one): near the kitchen door and, if available, one on every floor.
• In workplaces (NFPA 10): the travel distance to an extinguisher must not exceed 23 meters for Class A, and 9 or 15 meters for Class B (depending on the size of the extinguisher).
• The extinguisher sign must be visible from a distance.
• Hang the extinguisher on the wall at a height that is easy to reach (its top no higher than about 1.5 meters), not on the floor and not inside closed cupboards.`}],tips:["When buying: an ABC extinguisher you can lift and use (the weight is on the label) for the home, and a wet chemical extinguisher or a fire blanket for the kitchen","Before buying, check the label for the fire classes and the standard mark (such as EN 3)","Practice PASS in a training session with a training extinguisher","Read on the label how many seconds your extinguisher works for: most small extinguishers empty quickly","Take part in Civil Defense training when it is held in your neighborhood or workplace","Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller","Always keep your back toward the door"],warnings:["A small extinguisher only works for a few seconds: be ready","The nozzle of a CO₂ extinguisher becomes very cold: do not touch it","ABC powder on a computer can damage the equipment","A fire bigger than a trash can = get out, do not use an extinguisher","Lithium-ion batteries: ABC powder and CO₂ do not fully stop the fire. Cool the battery with plenty of water and call 115"],activity:{prompt:"You are at home. In the bedroom, a mobile phone charger plugged into an outlet has caught fire. The fire is small and you have 2 extinguishers within reach: a 2 kg ABC extinguisher and a wet chemical extinguisher. What will you do?",options:[{text:"First I cut off the electricity (I pull out the plug if it is safe, or I switch off the main power). I warn my family and call 115. Then, with my back toward the door, I take the ABC extinguisher and use PASS, starting from the distance shown on its label; if the fire does not go out at once, I get out and close the door.",correct:!0},{text:"I use the wet chemical extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1},{text:"I run away and do not tell anyone",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"In PASS, what does the letter P stand for?",options:[{text:"Push",correct:!1},{text:"Pull (pull out the safety pin)",correct:!0},{text:"Point",correct:!1},{text:"Press",correct:!1}]},{question:"Roughly how long does a small ABC powder extinguisher (2 kg) work for?",options:[{text:"3–5 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Only a few seconds; the exact time is on its label",correct:!0},{text:"1 minute",correct:!1},{text:"Without limit",correct:!1}]},{question:"How do you know how far from the fire to start?",options:[{text:"Always start half a meter away",correct:!1},{text:"From the extinguisher label: start at the distance shown there, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller",correct:!0},{text:"Always start 7–10 meters away",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}]},{question:"When should you not use the extinguisher, and get out instead?",options:[{text:"Never: always use it",correct:!1},{text:"When the fire is bigger than a trash can or the room is filling with smoke",correct:!0},{text:"Only in the kitchen",correct:!1},{text:"Only in the bedroom",correct:!1}]},{question:"Which extinguisher is suitable for a cooking oil fire?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"ABC powder",correct:!1},{text:"Wet chemical (Class K/F)",correct:!0},{text:"CO₂",correct:!1}]}]},{id:15,icon:"HeartHandshake",accent:"#e11d48",title:"First Aid for Citizens",subtitle:"Primary assessment, CPR, AED, bleeding, burns and choking",summary:"A person's life can be saved by an ordinary citizen who knows what to do. After cardiac arrest, every minute without CPR and without an AED reduces the chance of survival. Following the AHA 2025 guidelines, this lesson explains the primary assessment (DRABC), chest compressions (CPR), the automated external defibrillator (AED), the Heimlich maneuver, stopping bleeding, burns and some other emergencies. Every citizen should learn this information.",blocks:[{heading:"DRABC: The Primary Assessment",body:`• D = Danger: is there any danger, such as electricity, fire or traffic? Before anything else, protect your own safety.
• R = Response: tap their shoulders and ask loudly: "Are you all right?" If there is no response, call 122 and ask for an AED.
• A = Airway: tilt the head back and lift the chin.
• B = Breathing: for no more than 10 seconds, look to see whether the chest is moving and whether breathing is normal.
• C = CPR: if they are not breathing normally, start chest compressions immediately. Members of the public do not need to check for a pulse.`},{heading:"CPR: 30:2 or Hands-Only",body:`• Position: the center of the chest, one hand on top of the other, arms straight.
• Adults (including the elderly): depth 5–6 cm, rate 100–120 compressions per minute.
• 30 compressions + 2 breaths if you are trained; if not, give compressions only (Hands-Only). Children, infants and people who drowned especially need breaths: if you are trained and able, give CPR with breaths; if you cannot, compressions alone are better than nothing.
• Do not stop until the AED arrives, the ambulance team takes over, or you can no longer continue.
• Children (from one year old to puberty): with one or both hands, depth about 5 cm (one third of the depth of the chest).
• Infants (under one year): with both thumbs and the hands encircling the chest, or with the heel of one hand; depth about 4 cm. The two-finger method is no longer taught (AHA 2025).
• For children and infants, when two rescuers perform CPR together: 15:2.`},{heading:"AED: Automated External Defibrillator",body:`AEDs are found in public places such as government offices, hospitals and large shopping centers. Anyone can use one, because the device gives spoken instructions. Using it early greatly increases the chance of survival.
1. Switch the device on.
2. Place the 2 pads on the bare chest, as shown in the picture on the pads.
3. Listen to the device's voice instructions.
4. Call out: "Nobody touch the patient!"
5. If the device tells you to, press the shock button.
6. Immediately continue CPR for 2 minutes and follow the device's instructions.

• During the shock, no one should touch the casualty!`},{heading:"Heimlich: Choking",body:`Signs: the person cannot speak, cough or breathe, and holds their throat with their hands.
1. Give 5 blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades (back blows).
2. Then give 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich): place your fist slightly above the navel and well below the breastbone, and press quickly inward and upward.
3. Repeat 5+5 until the object comes out; if the person becomes unconscious, call 122 and start CPR.

• Infants (under one year): 5 back blows (head lower than the chest) + 5 chest thrusts. Never give abdominal thrusts.
• Pregnant women or very overweight people: give thrusts in the center of the chest, not on the abdomen.`},{heading:"Bleeding: Stop the Bleed",body:`1. Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly and continuously. If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another cloth on top.
2. Wound packing: if the wound is deep, pack clean wound gauze into the wound and then press firmly.
3. Tourniquet: for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg, apply it 5–7 cm above the wound (not over the knee or elbow) and tighten it until the bleeding stops. Write down the time it was applied, and never loosen or remove it: only the medical team removes it.

• Do not pull a sharp object out of a wound!
• Eye injury: do not press on the eye.
• Head injury: if a skull fracture is suspected, press only gently.
• Call 122.`},{heading:"Burns and Other Emergencies",body:`• Burns: 20 minutes of cool running water (not hot water and not ice).
• Fainting: lay the person on their back and raise their legs slightly.
• Person with diabetes: if they are conscious and able to swallow, give them something sweet.
• Panic attack: stay calm and help them to breathe slowly.
• Head or neck injury: do not move the person; keep them still and call 122. Move them only if the place is unsafe or they need CPR, keeping the head, neck and back in line. Do not put them on their side (recovery position).
• Fracture: do not move the limb; keep it still and place wrapped ice on the swelling.
• Electric shock: first cut off the electricity.
• Snake bite: move away from the snake and keep the person calm and still. Keep the bitten arm or leg still with a splint, and remove rings, watches and tight clothing before it swells. Call 122 and get the person to a hospital quickly. Do not cut or suck the wound, do not tie a tight band or tourniquet, do not put ice on it and do not use traditional or herbal remedies.
• Poisoning: call 122 and keep the container or label to show the doctors. Do not make the person vomit, and do not give milk, oil, salt water or other home remedies; give nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor tells you to. If kerosene, gasoline or lamp oil was swallowed, vomiting is especially dangerous because it can go into the lungs. If the poison is on the skin or in the eyes, rinse with plenty of running water and take off soiled clothing. If the person is drowsy or unconscious but breathing normally, lay them on their side.`}],tips:["122 for an ambulance and 115 for fire and rescue: memorize them","Take a CPR and first aid course, and renew it every few years","Know where the AEDs are near your home and your workplace","After cardiac arrest, every minute counts: start CPR quickly",`The song "Stayin' Alive" helps you remember the rhythm of 100–120 compressions per minute`,"Keep a first aid kit at home, in the car and at work","In the first aid kit: clean cloths, gloves, a tourniquet, scissors and a list of emergency numbers"],warnings:["Never touch a person who has had an electric shock until the electricity has been cut off","If a back or neck injury is suspected, do not move a person who has fallen, unless the place is unsafe or they need CPR","Tourniquet: write down the time it was applied, and never loosen or remove it","Do not put hot water, ice, butter or toothpaste on a burn","AED pads must not overlap; for children under 8 years old, use child pads if available"],activity:{prompt:"On the street, you see a 55-year-old person collapse. They are not responding and not breathing. There is an AED at the entrance of a nearby government office, and another person is there. What will you do?",options:[{text:"I make sure the area is safe and check for a response. I call 122 and send the other person to bring the AED. I start CPR (30:2) immediately and use the AED as soon as it arrives.",correct:!0},{text:"I only send someone to bring the AED and wait without doing CPR",correct:!1},{text:"I give them water",correct:!1},{text:"I record a video from a distance",correct:!1}]},quiz:[{question:"In CPR, what is the compression depth for adults?",options:[{text:"2 cm",correct:!1},{text:"5–6 cm",correct:!0},{text:"10 cm",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}]},{question:"Who can use an AED?",options:[{text:"Only doctors",correct:!1},{text:"Anyone: the device gives spoken instructions",correct:!0},{text:"Only firefighters",correct:!1},{text:"No one outside a hospital",correct:!1}]},{question:"How is CPR performed on infants (under one year)?",options:[{text:"With both hands, as for adults",correct:!1},{text:"With both thumbs (hands encircling the chest) or the heel of one hand, depth about 4 cm",correct:!0},{text:"With two fingers, 2 cm",correct:!1},{text:"CPR is not performed on infants",correct:!1}]},{question:"After applying a tourniquet, what should you do?",options:[{text:"I loosen it after 10 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"I write down the time it was applied and never loosen it before the medical team arrives",correct:!0},{text:"I release it every half hour and tighten it again",correct:!1},{text:"I remove it as soon as the bleeding stops",correct:!1}]},{question:"An infant under one year old is choking on something and is still conscious. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Abdominal thrusts (Heimlich) immediately",correct:!1},{text:"5 back blows + 5 chest thrusts",correct:!0},{text:"CPR immediately",correct:!1},{text:"I leave them alone",correct:!1}]}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
